#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# inverse

#% text
# `inverse(m)` gives the inverse of the square matrix `m`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The inverse is the matrix `w` with `dot(m, w)` equal to the identity matrix.

#% item
# The entries may be exact numbers, symbolic expressions, machine numbers or balls.

#% item
# A singular matrix gives `failure(singular, inverse)`; a matrix that is not square gives `failure(not_square, inverse)`.

#% item
# To solve `dot(m, x) == b`, `linear_solve(m, b)` is faster and more accurate than forming the inverse.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
inverse([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[-2, 1], [3/2, -1/2]]

#% text
# A diagonal matrix:

#% cell
inverse([[2, 0, 0], [0, 4, 0], [0, 0, 5]])
#% output
#= [[1/2, 0, 0], [0, 1/4, 0], [0, 0, 1/5]]

#% text
# A symbolic matrix:

#% cell
inverse([[x, 1], [1, x]])
#% output
#= [[x/(-1 + x^2), -1/(-1 + x^2)], [-1/(-1 + x^2), x/(-1 + x^2)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Machine numbers:

#% cell
inverse([[1.0, 2.0], [3.0, 4.0]])
#% output
#= [[-1.9999999999999998, 1.0], [1.4999999999999998, -0.49999999999999994]]

#% text
# Balls:

#% cell
inverse(n([[1, 2], [3, 4]], 20))
#% output
#= [[-2.00000000000000000000000, 1.0000000000000000000000], [1.50000000000000000000000, -0.500000000000000000000000]]

#% text
# Complex entries:

#% cell
inverse([[1, im], [0, 1]])
#% output
#= [[1, -im], [0, 1]]

#% text
# The 3 x 3 Hilbert matrix has an inverse with integer entries:

#% cell
inverse(table(1/(i + j - 1), i, 3, j, 3))
#% output
#= [[9, -36, 30], [-36, 192, -180], [30, -180, 180]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A matrix times its inverse is the identity:

#% cell
m = [[2, 1], [7, 3]]; dot(m, inverse(m))
#% output
#= [[1, 0], [0, 1]]

#% text
# The inverse of the inverse is the matrix:

#% cell
inverse(inverse(m)) == m
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `linear_solve` with the identity matrix gives the inverse:

#% cell
linear_solve(m, identity_matrix(2)) == inverse(m)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A singular matrix has no inverse:

#% cell
inverse([[1, 2], [2, 4]])
#% output
#! failure(singular, inverse)

#% text
# A nearly singular machine matrix has a huge inverse, which changes greatly with tiny changes of the entries:

#% cell
inverse([[1.0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0 + 1e-15]])
#% output
#= [[900719925474100.3, -900719925474099.3], [-900719925474099.3, 900719925474099.3]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `linear_solve`, `det`, `dot`, `identity_matrix`, `lu`
